#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Break code briefly and see whether a command catches it, then put the code back.

  mutate-check -- uv run pytest -q tests/test_watch.py -k seen_before <<'EDITS'
  --- src/app/watch.py
  <<<<<<< SEARCH
                  and p.listing.id not in sketchy
  =======
  >>>>>>> REPLACE
  EDITS

  mutate-check --diff main -- uv run pytest -q tests/test_watch.py

  mutate-check [--timeout SECONDS] [--edits FILE] -- CMD...
  mutate-check --diff BASE [--max N] [--timeout SECONDS] -- CMD...

Pick a heredoc delimiter that isn't a line of the blocks: a line EOF in the text ends
<<'EOF' early, and the shell runs the rest as commands.

Stdin holds apply-edits blocks (`apply-edits -h` shows the format), which apply-edits
applies all or nothing; --edits FILE reads them from FILE instead, and stdin isn't
read. Use --edits under bgrun or heavy: bgrun gives its job no stdin, so `bgrun ... --
heavy -- mutate-check -- CMD < blocks` runs with no blocks. Then the command runs, and
every file the blocks name is put back byte for byte, also when mutate-check is
stopped by Ctrl-C, SIGTERM or SIGHUP.

A failure counts as a catch only when tests ran. A mutation that stops the build (a Rust
`error: could not compile` or `error[E...]`, as unused code under `-D warnings` does),
pytest exiting 2, 4 or 5 (collection errors, usage, no tests) with no `N failed` in
its summary (under pytest-xdist, -x exits 2 after a failure), or a `cargo test` or
`cargo nextest` run that fails with no line saying a test ran, is inconclusive: no test
judged it. So is a command that runs past --timeout (default 600 seconds), which is
stopped: a hang isn't a catch. A cargo test run that passes with no test run (a
filter that matched none) is inconclusive too.

The cargo case covers nextest refusing a filter (exit 94) or finding no test to run
(exit 4). A test ran when the output, colour codes aside, has libtest's `test result:`,
nextest's `Summary [...]` with a count of tests run other than 0, or cargo's `error:
test failed, to rerun pass ...` (also `doctest`, `bench`), which it prints for a test
binary that crashed or has no libtest harness. The command is a cargo test run when it
holds `cargo`, then any `+toolchain` or `-flag` words, then `test`, `t` or `nextest`,
also inside `sh -c '...'` or after a wrapper such as `heavy --`.

Prints one line, with up to 3 of the command's pytest `E ` lines or Rust `error` lines
when it failed, or its last line of output when there are none; never the whole output.

Stopped, or past --timeout, it stops the command's process group: SIGTERM, then
SIGKILL after $MUTATE_CHECK_GRACE seconds (default 5; the tests shorten it).

The command runs with PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1. Python takes a .pyc as current when
its source's size and mtime in whole seconds match, so a same-size mutation put back
within the second would leave its bytecode for the next import. A Python started with
-E or -I ignores the variable, so each file put back also gets an mtime at least a
second past the mutant's.

Exit status: 0 the command failed, so the mutation was caught; 1 it passed, so the
tests miss it; 2 usage error, an --edits FILE that can't be read, or blocks that didn't
apply (the command didn't run);
3 inconclusive: the build failed, no tests ran, or the command hung; 128+N stopped by
signal N.

--diff BASE makes the mutants itself, from the lines added or changed since BASE's
merge base with HEAD, work tree included: commits since BASE, and edits not yet
committed. Not a moved file's unchanged lines, and not untracked files, which it names
(`git add -N FILE` brings one in). It reads Python files (.py, or no extension and a
python or `uv run --script` shebang) and shell scripts (.sh, .bash, or an sh, bash or
dash shebang). Tests and their helpers are left alone: conftest.py, and a folder or a
file stem such as tests, test_x, x_test, x_testing or test-x. On those lines:

  Python  a simple statement becomes `pass`; an if, elif or while condition is
          negated; each operand of an and/or is dropped in turn; ==, is and in swap
          with !=, is not and not in; True and False swap; a string becomes
          'XX<string>XX', unless it holds whitespace (a message), a URL or a digest,
          or sits in a docstring, an annotation, an f-string or a list, tuple, set
          or dict at a module's top level (a data table).
  shell   a command becomes `:`, and a `cmd || {` group up to its `}` too; an if,
          elif, while or until condition is negated; = or == and !=, -eq and -ne
          swap inside [ ] or [[ ]]; W in ${N:-W}, ${N-W}, ${N:=W} or ${N=W} becomes
          XXWXX.

Left out as changing nothing a test sees: a return of nothing that ends a function, a
continue that ends a loop, a final `exit 0`, True and False in a decorator (as
`@dataclass(frozen=True)`) or as `flush=`, and the conditions of `if __name__ ==
"__main__"` and `if TYPE_CHECKING`. A mutant that doesn't compile (Python's compile,
bash -n) is skipped, and two that read the same run once.

CMD runs unmutated first, and unless that run reads as a pass with tests run, so
that a mutant could be judged, no mutant runs: it prints what the run did, such as
failing, hanging, or a cargo test run that ran no test. If it rewrites a code file,
mutate-check puts the file back and stops. Then CMD runs once per mutant, judged as
above, up to --max N (default 40), and if the first 3 are all inconclusive, it stops
there and prints the last one's verdict: each statement's first mutant
before any statement's second, and last, deleting a message (a call whose arguments
are all f-strings or strings with spaces, an echo to stderr) or a shell setting
(set, shopt). Each run stops after --timeout, or else ten times the unmutated run
and at least 30 seconds ($MUTATE_CHECK_MIN_TIMEOUT; the tests shorten it). CMD's
stdin is /dev/null, here as in the other mode.

It prints a line for each mutant that survived, `FILE:LINE: what changed`, then
`mutate-check: N caught, N survived, N inconclusive, N skipped (didn't parse)`, and
how many --max left out; run again with a higher --max to try those. CMD runs up to
N+1 times, so run it as a background call under heavy, with the edits to the tree
done: `bgrun --wait -o LOG -- heavy -- mutate-check --diff BASE -- CMD`.

Exit status with --diff: 0 no mutant survived; 1 one did; 2 usage error, BASE isn't
a commit, CMD rewrote a code file, or a file couldn't be put back; 3 no mutant was
judged: the unmutated run didn't pass with tests run, the first 3 mutants or all of
them were inconclusive or skipped, or a changed code file couldn't be read and the
rest had no mutants; 128+N stopped by signal N, after putting the file back.
"""

import ast
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import itertools
import math
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import warnings
from collections.abc import Iterator
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path, PurePosixPath
from types import FrameType, ModuleType

CAUGHT, MISSED, USAGE, INCONCLUSIVE = 0, 1, 2, 3
DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 600.0
# pytest's statuses when no test judged anything: interrupted or collection errors,
# a usage error, no tests collected.
PYTEST_NOTHING_RAN = {2, 4, 5}
# pytest's summary line when a test failed: under pytest-xdist, -x then exits 2.
PYTEST_FAILED = re.compile(r"\b[1-9]\d* failed\b.* in \d+(\.\d+)?s\b")
BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SHOWN_ERRORS = 3
STOP_SIGNALS = {signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP}


class UsageError(Exception):
    pass


class Stopped(Exception):
    def __init__(self, signum: int) -> None:
        self.signum = signum


def apply_edits() -> ModuleType:
    """bin/apply-edits as a module, for its parser: the one place the format lives."""
    path = str(BIN / "apply-edits")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("apply_edits", path)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("apply_edits", loader)
    assert spec is not None
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


FORMAT = apply_edits()


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Original:
    content: bytes
    mode: int


def snapshot(paths: list[Path]) -> dict[Path, Original]:
    """The files' bytes and permissions, by their real paths."""
    originals = {}
    for path in paths:
        target = path.resolve()
        if target.is_file():
            originals[target] = Original(target.read_bytes(), target.stat().st_mode)
    return originals


def put(target: Path, original: Original) -> None:
    """Write the file through a temp file beside it, so a crash can't leave half of one."""
    fd, temp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=target.parent, prefix=f".{target.name}.")
    with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
        f.write(original.content)
    os.chmod(temp, original.mode)
    os.replace(temp, target)


def restore(originals: dict[Path, Original]) -> list[str]:
    """Put each file back as it was, also when the command deleted it. Its mtime moves
    on by at least a second, so no .pyc written for the mutant passes for its own.
    Signals wait until it's done. Returns what couldn't be put back."""
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, STOP_SIGNALS)
    failed = []
    for target, original in originals.items():
        try:
            mutated = target.stat().st_mtime_ns if target.exists() else 0
            put(target, original)
            later = max(time.time_ns(), mutated + 1_000_000_000)
            os.utime(target, ns=(later, later))
        except OSError as e:
            failed.append(f"mutate-check: couldn't put back {target}: {e}")
    return failed


class TimedOut(Exception):
    pass


def run(command: list[str], timeout: float) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """The command's exit status and output. Stopped, or past `timeout` seconds (then
    raising TimedOut), it stops the command too, killing it if it ignores SIGTERM for 5
    seconds ($MUTATE_CHECK_GRACE, which the tests shorten)."""
    # Read before the command starts, so a bad value stops here rather than mid-stop
    grace = float(os.environ.get("MUTATE_CHECK_GRACE", "5"))
    # Its own process group, so stopping it stops what it started, such as the test
    # binaries cargo runs, too. PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE: the docstring says why.
    child = subprocess.Popen(
        command,
        stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
        stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
        start_new_session=True,
        env=os.environ | {"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE": "1"},
    )
    try:
        output, _ = child.communicate(timeout=timeout)
    except Stopped:
        stop_child(child, grace)
        raise
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
        stop_child(child, grace)
        raise TimedOut from e
    # Without colour codes, which CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always or --color puts mid-line
    return child.returncode, COLOUR.sub("", output.decode(errors="replace"))


def stop_child(child: subprocess.Popen[bytes], grace: float) -> None:
    """Stop the command's process group: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL once `grace` seconds
    pass, or at once when the command has gone but left children behind."""
    signal_group(child, signal.SIGTERM)
    try:
        child.wait(timeout=grace)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        pass
    signal_group(child, signal.SIGKILL)
    child.wait()


def signal_group(child: subprocess.Popen[bytes], signum: signal.Signals) -> None:
    try:
        os.killpg(child.pid, signum)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pass


RUST_BUILD_FAILED = re.compile(r"error: could not compile|error\[E\d+\]")
# A lint that `-D warnings` makes an error: the mutation left an item unused.
RUST_UNUSED = re.compile(
    r"is never (used|read|constructed)|unused `|unused (variable|import)|does not need to be mutable"
)
# A compile error's own line: `error: ...` or `error[E0308]: ...`, or with
# --message-format=short `src/lib.rs:4:5: error: ...`.
RUST_ERROR = re.compile(r"^error(\[E\d+\])?: |^\S+:\d+:\d+: error")
# cargo's and nextest's lines that only say the build failed, after the cause.
RUST_FAILED_ONLY = re.compile(r"^error: (could not compile|command `)")
UNUSED_HINT = (
    "  hint: the mutation left code unused, which -D warnings refuses; keep it used,"
    " such as with `let _ = &name;`, or mutate a condition instead of removing a call"
)
# A line that says tests ran, so a failure is theirs: libtest's summary, or its line
# before the tests, which is all a binary that crashed under a test prints; nextest's
# Summary when it counts a test run (it prints `0 tests run` before exiting 4 for no
# tests). Not cargo's `error: test failed`, which follows libtest refusing an option.
TESTS_RAN = re.compile(
    r"^test result: |^running \d+ tests?$|^\s*Summary \[[^\]]*\]\s*[1-9]"
)
# A line that says a test ran and passed: libtest's summary, or nextest's.
TESTS_PASSED = re.compile(
    r"^test result: \w+\. [1-9]\d* passed|^\s*Summary \[[^\]]*\]\s*[1-9]\d* tests? run"
)
COLOUR = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")
# `cargo`, then any `+toolchain` or `-flag` words, then a subcommand that runs tests,
# anywhere in the command, so also inside `sh -c '...'` or after `heavy --`.
CARGO_TEST_RUN = re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])cargo(\s+[-+]\S*)*\s+(test|t|nextest)(?![\w-])")


def tests_ran(lines: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(TESTS_RAN.search(line) for line in lines)


def rust_build_failed(lines: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(RUST_BUILD_FAILED.match(line) for line in lines)


def rust_errors(lines: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Rust's error lines: each error and the `-->` line that places it, causes first,
    then cargo's lines that only say the build failed, so the cap on lines shown keeps
    the cause."""
    causes, failed = [], []
    for index, line in enumerate(lines):
        if not RUST_ERROR.match(line):
            continue
        if RUST_FAILED_ONLY.match(line):
            failed.append(line)
            continue
        causes.append(line)
        place = lines[index + 1].strip() if index + 1 < len(lines) else ""
        if place.startswith("-->"):
            causes.append(f"  {place}")
    return causes + failed


def runs_pytest(command: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(Path(arg).name == "pytest" or arg.endswith("pytest") for arg in command)


def runs_cargo_tests(command: list[str]) -> bool:
    return CARGO_TEST_RUN.search(" ".join(command)) is not None


def summary(output: str) -> list[str]:
    """Up to SHOWN_ERRORS of pytest's `E ` lines, else the last non-empty line."""
    lines = output.splitlines()
    errors = [line for line in lines if line.startswith("E ")]
    if errors:
        return errors[:SHOWN_ERRORS]
    last = next((line for line in reversed(lines) if line.strip()), None)
    return [last] if last is not None else []


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Verdict:
    """What a run with a mutation in place says: CAUGHT, MISSED or INCONCLUSIVE, the
    lines that report it, and what the run did, said of an unmutated run."""

    status: int
    report: list[str]
    reason: str


def judge(code: int, output: str, command: list[str]) -> Verdict:
    """The verdict on the command's run with a mutation in place: a failure is a catch
    only when tests ran, and a pass is a miss only when one could have."""
    lines = output.splitlines()
    if (
        code == 0
        and runs_cargo_tests(command)
        and not any(map(TESTS_PASSED.search, lines))
    ):
        report = ["mutate-check: inconclusive: the command passed, but no test ran"]
        return Verdict(INCONCLUSIVE, report, "it passed, but no test ran")
    if code == 0:
        report = ["mutate-check: not caught: the command passed with the mutation"]
        return Verdict(MISSED, report, "it passed")
    # A Rust build that failed, or a cargo test run, with no line saying a test ran
    no_rust_test = not tests_ran(lines) and (
        runs_cargo_tests(command) or rust_build_failed(lines)
    )
    errors = rust_errors(lines) if no_rust_test else []
    pytest_ran_none = (
        runs_pytest(command)
        and code in PYTEST_NOTHING_RAN
        and not any(map(PYTEST_FAILED.search, lines))
    )
    if no_rust_test or pytest_ran_none:
        report = [
            (
                f"mutate-check: inconclusive: the command exited {code} before any"
                " test judged the mutation"
            ),
            *(f"  {line}" for line in (errors or summary(output))[:SHOWN_ERRORS]),
        ]
        if any(RUST_UNUSED.search(line) for line in errors):
            report.append(UNUSED_HINT)
        return Verdict(INCONCLUSIVE, report, f"it exited {code} before any test ran")
    return Verdict(
        CAUGHT,
        [
            f"mutate-check: caught: the command exited {code}",
            *(f"  {line}" for line in summary(output)),
        ],
        f"it exited {code}",
    )


def stop(signum: int, _frame: FrameType | None) -> None:
    raise Stopped(signum)


def mutated_run(edits: str, command: list[str], timeout: float) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Apply the edits, run the command, and give its status and output. Raises
    Stopped, and UsageError when the edits don't apply or the command won't start."""
    applied = subprocess.run(
        [str(BIN / "apply-edits")],
        input=edits,
        text=True,
        capture_output=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if applied.returncode != 0:
        raise UsageError(applied.stderr.rstrip())
    try:
        return run(command, timeout)
    except OSError as e:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: can't run {command[0]}: {e}") from e


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Options:
    command: list[str]
    timeout: float | None = None
    edits_file: Path | None = None
    base: str | None = None
    limit: int | None = None


# --diff BASE: mutants made from the Python and shell lines a diff adds or changes.

DEFAULT_MAX = 40
EARLY_STOP = 3
TIMEOUT_FACTOR = 10
MIN_TIMEOUT = 30.0
SHOWN_SOURCE = 72
PYTHON, SHELL = "Python", "shell"
# A folder, or a file's stem, that holds tests or their helpers.
TEST_PART = re.compile(r"(^|[_-])tests?(ing)?([_-]|$)")
HUNK = re.compile(r"@@ -\S+ \+(\d+)(?:,\d+)? @@")
# A mutant's place among its statement's mutants follows its operator's.
DELETE, NEGATE, DROP, SWAP, BOOL, STRING, DELETE_NAMES = range(7)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Mutant:
    """A change to one file: its bytes from `start` to `end` become `new`."""

    path: Path
    line: int
    # Where its statement starts: each statement's first mutant runs before any second.
    group: int
    # Its operator, then its column: its place among its statement's mutants.
    rank: tuple[int, int]
    # It deletes a message or a setting, so it runs after every other mutant.
    late: bool
    start: int
    end: int
    new: bytes
    description: str

    def applied_to(self, text: bytes) -> bytes:
        return text[: self.start] + self.new + text[self.end :]


class NotMutated(Exception):
    """Why a code file has no mutants."""


def short(source: str) -> str:
    """Source on one line, cut to SHOWN_SOURCE characters."""
    flat = " ".join(source.split())
    return flat if len(flat) <= SHOWN_SOURCE else flat[: SHOWN_SOURCE - 3] + "..."


def line_starts(text: bytes) -> list[int]:
    """The byte offset where each line starts, then the text's length."""
    starts = [0]
    for line in text.splitlines(keepends=True):
        starts.append(starts[-1] + len(line))
    return starts


SIMPLE_STATEMENTS = (
    ast.Expr,
    ast.Assign,
    ast.AugAssign,
    ast.AnnAssign,
    ast.Return,
    ast.Raise,
    ast.Delete,
    ast.Continue,
)
FUNCTIONS = (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)
OPPOSITE = {
    ast.Eq: ast.NotEq,
    ast.NotEq: ast.Eq,
    ast.Is: ast.IsNot,
    ast.IsNot: ast.Is,
    ast.In: ast.NotIn,
    ast.NotIn: ast.In,
}
SYMBOL = {
    ast.Eq: "==",
    ast.NotEq: "!=",
    ast.Is: "is",
    ast.IsNot: "is not",
    ast.In: "in",
    ast.NotIn: "not in",
}
# What an and/or's remaining operand needs brackets for, where the and/or stood.
LOOSE = (ast.IfExp, ast.Lambda, ast.NamedExpr)
DISPLAYS = (ast.List, ast.Tuple, ast.Set, ast.Dict)
HEX_DIGEST = re.compile(r"[0-9a-fA-F]{32}")


class PythonFile:
    """A Python file's text and syntax tree, and the mutants of its changed lines."""

    def __init__(self, path: Path, text: bytes, changed: set[int]) -> None:
        try:
            text.decode()
        except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
            raise NotMutated("isn't UTF-8") from e
        try:
            with warnings.catch_warnings():
                warnings.simplefilter("ignore")
                self.tree = ast.parse(text)
        except (SyntaxError, ValueError) as e:
            raise NotMutated("doesn't parse") from e
        self.path, self.text, self.changed = path, text, changed
        self.starts = line_starts(text)
        self.parents = {
            id(child): node
            for node in ast.walk(self.tree)
            for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(node)
        }
        self.left_alone = left_alone(self.tree)
        self.decorators = {
            id(node)
            for definition in ast.walk(self.tree)
            if isinstance(definition, (*FUNCTIONS, ast.ClassDef))
            for decorator in definition.decorator_list
            for node in ast.walk(decorator)
        }
        self.table_strings = {
            id(node)
            for statement in self.tree.body
            if not isinstance(statement, (*FUNCTIONS, ast.ClassDef))
            for display in ast.walk(statement)
            if isinstance(display, DISPLAYS)
            for node in ast.walk(display)
            if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str)
        }

    def mutants(self) -> list[Mutant]:
        return [
            mutant
            for node in ast.walk(self.tree)
            if id(node) not in self.left_alone
            for operator in PYTHON_OPERATORS
            for mutant in operator(self, node)
        ]

    def touched(self, node: ast.AST) -> bool:
        return not self.changed.isdisjoint(range(node.lineno, node.end_lineno + 1))

    def offset(self, line: int, col: int) -> int:
        return self.starts[line - 1] + col

    def source(self, node: ast.AST) -> str:
        start = self.offset(node.lineno, node.col_offset)
        return self.text[
            start : self.offset(node.end_lineno, node.end_col_offset)
        ].decode()

    def parent(self, node: ast.AST) -> ast.AST | None:
        return self.parents.get(id(node))

    def ends_its_block(self, node: ast.stmt) -> bool:
        """A return of nothing that ends a function, or a continue that ends a loop:
        deleting it changes nothing."""
        parent = self.parent(node)
        if isinstance(node, ast.Return) and is_none(node.value):
            return isinstance(parent, FUNCTIONS) and parent.body[-1] is node
        if isinstance(node, ast.Continue):
            loops = (ast.For, ast.AsyncFor, ast.While)
            return isinstance(parent, loops) and parent.body[-1] is node
        return False

    def mutant(
        self,
        span: ast.AST,
        rank: int,
        new: str,
        description: str,
        at: ast.AST | None = None,
        late: bool = False,
    ) -> Mutant:
        """`span`'s text becomes `new`; `at` (default `span`) places it."""
        at = at or span
        statement = span
        while not isinstance(statement, ast.stmt):
            statement = self.parents[id(statement)]
        return Mutant(
            self.path,
            at.lineno,
            self.offset(statement.lineno, statement.col_offset),
            (rank, at.col_offset),
            late,
            self.offset(span.lineno, span.col_offset),
            self.offset(span.end_lineno, span.end_col_offset),
            new.encode(),
            description,
        )


def left_alone(tree: ast.Module) -> set[int]:
    """The nodes no operator touches: annotations, f-strings, statements of a lone
    constant (docstrings), and the conditions of `if __name__ == "__main__"` and `if
    TYPE_CHECKING`."""
    roots: list[ast.AST | None] = []
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.arg):
            roots.append(node.annotation)
        elif isinstance(node, FUNCTIONS):
            roots.append(node.returns)
        elif isinstance(node, ast.AnnAssign):
            roots.append(node.annotation)
        elif isinstance(node, ast.JoinedStr) or (
            isinstance(node, ast.Expr) and isinstance(node.value, ast.Constant)
        ):
            roots.append(node)
        elif isinstance(node, ast.If) and (
            is_main_check(node.test) or names_type_checking(node.test)
        ):
            roots.append(node.test)
    return {id(node) for root in roots if root is not None for node in ast.walk(root)}


def is_main_check(test: ast.expr) -> bool:
    return (
        isinstance(test, ast.Compare)
        and isinstance(test.left, ast.Name)
        and test.left.id == "__name__"
    )


def names_type_checking(test: ast.expr) -> bool:
    return any(
        (isinstance(node, ast.Name) and node.id == "TYPE_CHECKING")
        or (isinstance(node, ast.Attribute) and node.attr == "TYPE_CHECKING")
        for node in ast.walk(test)
    )


def is_none(node: ast.expr | None) -> bool:
    return node is None or (isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and node.value is None)


def is_prose(node: ast.expr) -> bool:
    """An f-string, or a string with whitespace in it: a message, not a value."""
    return isinstance(node, ast.JoinedStr) or (
        isinstance(node, ast.Constant)
        and isinstance(node.value, str)
        and any(c.isspace() for c in node.value)
    )


def says_a_message(node: ast.stmt) -> bool:
    """A call such as `say(f"...")`, whose arguments are all messages."""
    call = node.value if isinstance(node, ast.Expr) else None
    return (
        isinstance(call, ast.Call) and bool(call.args) and all(map(is_prose, call.args))
    )


def opposite(test: ast.expr) -> ast.expr:
    """The condition negated: a lone ==, is or in comparison takes the opposite
    operator."""
    if is_lone_swappable(test):
        return swapped(test, 0)
    return ast.UnaryOp(ast.Not(), test)


def is_lone_swappable(node: ast.AST) -> bool:
    """A comparison with one operator, and that one ==, !=, is, is not, in or not in."""
    return (
        isinstance(node, ast.Compare)
        and len(node.ops) == 1
        and type(node.ops[0]) in OPPOSITE
    )


def swapped(node: ast.Compare, index: int) -> ast.Compare:
    ops = list(node.ops)
    ops[index] = OPPOSITE[type(ops[index])]()
    return ast.Compare(node.left, ops, node.comparators)


def deleted(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """A simple statement becomes `pass`."""
    if not isinstance(node, SIMPLE_STATEMENTS) or not f.touched(node):
        return
    if (isinstance(node, ast.AnnAssign) and node.value is None) or f.ends_its_block(
        node
    ):
        return
    targets = (
        node.targets
        if isinstance(node, ast.Assign)
        else [node.target]
        if isinstance(node, ast.AnnAssign)
        else []
    )
    # Deleting an assignment to names mostly raises NameError: other mutants first.
    names_only = bool(targets) and all(isinstance(t, ast.Name) for t in targets)
    yield f.mutant(
        node,
        DELETE_NAMES if names_only else DELETE,
        "pass",
        f"deleted: {short(f.source(node))}",
        late=says_a_message(node),
    )


def negated(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """The condition of an if, elif or while is negated."""
    if not isinstance(node, (ast.If, ast.While)) or not f.touched(node.test):
        return
    if id(node.test) in f.left_alone:
        return
    yield f.mutant(
        node.test,
        NEGATE,
        ast.unparse(opposite(node.test)),
        f"negated: {short(f.source(node.test))}",
    )


def dropped_operand(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """Each operand of an and/or is dropped in turn. Each drop that survives is a gap
    the and/or swap would miss when the other drop is caught."""
    if not isinstance(node, ast.BoolOp):
        return
    word = "and" if isinstance(node.op, ast.And) else "or"
    for index, value in enumerate(node.values):
        if not f.touched(value):
            continue
        rest = node.values[:index] + node.values[index + 1 :]
        kept = rest[0] if len(rest) == 1 else ast.BoolOp(node.op, rest)
        new = ast.unparse(kept)
        yield f.mutant(
            node,
            DROP,
            f"({new})" if isinstance(kept, LOOSE) else new,
            f"dropped from the {word}: {short(f.source(value))}",
            at=value,
        )


def swapped_comparison(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """==, is and in swap with !=, is not and not in. Where the comparison is a whole
    condition, its negation makes the same text, which runs once."""
    if not isinstance(node, ast.Compare) or not f.touched(node):
        return
    for index, op in enumerate(node.ops):
        if type(op) in OPPOSITE:
            yield f.mutant(
                node,
                SWAP,
                ast.unparse(swapped(node, index)),
                f"{SYMBOL[type(op)]} -> {SYMBOL[OPPOSITE[type(op)]]}:"
                f" {short(f.source(node))}",
            )


def swapped_bool(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """True and False swap, but not in a decorator, as `@dataclass(frozen=True)`, or as
    `flush=`: no test sees those."""
    if not (isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, bool)):
        return
    parent = f.parent(node)
    keyword = parent.arg if isinstance(parent, ast.keyword) else None
    if not f.touched(node) or id(node) in f.decorators or keyword == "flush":
        return
    old, new = str(node.value), str(not node.value)
    name = f"{keyword}=" if keyword else ""
    yield f.mutant(node, BOOL, new, f"{name}{old} -> {name}{new}")


def changed_string(f: PythonFile, node: ast.AST) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
    """A string that's a value, not a message, a URL, a digest or a module's data
    table, becomes 'XX<string>XX'."""
    if not (isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str)):
        return
    value = node.value
    if not f.touched(node) or id(node) in f.table_strings:
        return
    if any(c.isspace() for c in value) or "://" in value or HEX_DIGEST.search(value):
        return
    new = repr(f"XX{value}XX")
    yield f.mutant(node, STRING, new, f"{short(f.source(node))} -> {new}")


PYTHON_OPERATORS = (
    deleted,
    negated,
    dropped_operand,
    swapped_comparison,
    swapped_bool,
    changed_string,
)


def python_parses(text: bytes) -> bool:
    """Whether Python compiles the text: syntax, and what the symbol table checks."""
    try:
        with warnings.catch_warnings():
            warnings.simplefilter("ignore")
            compile(text, "<mutant>", "exec", dont_inherit=True)
    except (SyntaxError, ValueError):
        return False
    return True


BASH = shutil.which("bash")
SHELL_CONDITION = re.compile(
    r"(\s*(?:if|elif|while|until)\s+)(.*?)(\s*;\s*(?:then|do)\b.*)?"
)
SHELL_KEYWORDS = {"if", "then", "elif", "else", "fi", "for", "select", "while", "until"}
SHELL_KEYWORDS |= {"do", "done", "case", "in", "esac", "function", "{", "}", "(", ")"}
# A line that opens a compound command: a function, a loop, a group.
SHELL_OPENERS = {"{", "(", "then", "do", "else", "in"}
CASE_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\s*\(?[^\s()|;]+(\s*\|\s*[^\s()|;]+)*\)")
HEREDOC = re.compile(r"(?<!<)<<-?\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_]\w*)\1")
GUARD_GROUP = re.compile(r"(\|\||&&)\s*\{$")
SHELL_TEST = re.compile(r"\[\[?\s.*?\s\]\]?")
SHELL_COMPARISON = re.compile(r"(?<=\s)(==|!=|=|-eq|-ne)(?=\s)")
SHELL_OPPOSITE = {"==": "!=", "=": "!=", "!=": "=", "-eq": "-ne", "-ne": "-eq"}
SHELL_DEFAULT = re.compile(r"\$\{\w+:?[-=]([^}]+)\}")
# Settings, and messages to stderr: deleting them runs last.
SHELL_LATE = re.compile(r"(set|shopt)\s|(echo|printf)\s.*>&2")
CONTINUED = ("\\", "|", "&&")


class ShellFile:
    """A shell script's lines, and the mutants of its changed ones."""

    def __init__(self, path: Path, text: bytes, changed: set[int]) -> None:
        if BASH is None:
            raise NotMutated("no bash to check shell mutants")
        try:
            lines = [line.decode() for line in text.splitlines(keepends=True)]
        except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
            raise NotMutated("isn't UTF-8") from e
        if not shell_parses(text):
            raise NotMutated("doesn't parse")
        self.path, self.changed = path, changed
        self.lines = [line.rstrip("\r\n") for line in lines]
        self.starts = line_starts(text)

    def offset(self, index: int, col: int) -> int:
        """The byte offset of character `col` of line `index`, counted from 0."""
        return self.starts[index] + len(self.lines[index][:col].encode())

    def commands(self) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
        """Each command's first and last line, counted from 0, with continued lines
        joined. Comments, blank lines and heredocs are left out."""
        found, index = [], 0
        while index < len(self.lines):
            first = index
            while index + 1 < len(self.lines) and continues(self.lines[index]):
                index += 1
            heredocs = HEREDOC.findall("\n".join(self.lines[first : index + 1]))
            text = self.lines[first].strip()
            if text and not text.startswith("#") and not heredocs:
                found.append((first, index))
            index += 1
            for _, word in heredocs:
                while index < len(self.lines) and self.lines[index].strip() != word:
                    index += 1
                index += 1
        return found

    def mutants(self) -> list[Mutant]:
        commands = self.commands()
        return [
            mutant
            for number, (first, last) in enumerate(commands)
            if not self.changed.isdisjoint(range(first + 1, last + 2))
            for mutant in self.command_mutants(first, last, number == len(commands) - 1)
        ]

    def command_mutants(self, first: int, last: int, ends_file: bool) -> list[Mutant]:
        found = list(self.defaults(first, last))
        line = self.lines[first]
        condition = SHELL_CONDITION.fullmatch(line) if first == last else None
        if condition:
            return [self.negation(first, condition), *found]
        text = self.text(first, last)
        words = text.split()
        if words[0] in SHELL_KEYWORDS or text.startswith(("}", ")", ";;")):
            return found
        if CASE_PATTERN.match(line) or (ends_file and text == "exit 0"):
            return found
        if GUARD_GROUP.search(text):
            end = self.group_end(last)
            if end is not None:
                found.append(self.deletion(first, end, len(self.lines[end])))
            return found
        if words[-1] in SHELL_OPENERS:
            return found
        content = self.lines[last].rstrip()
        end_col = len(content.removesuffix(";;").rstrip())
        found.append(self.deletion(first, last, end_col))
        if first == last:
            found += self.comparisons(first)
        return found

    def text(self, first: int, last: int) -> str:
        parts = self.lines[first : last + 1]
        return " ".join(part.strip().removesuffix("\\").strip() for part in parts)

    def group_end(self, opener: int) -> int | None:
        """The line that closes the `{` group `opener` ends with."""
        depth = 1
        for index in range(opener + 1, len(self.lines)):
            text = self.lines[index].strip()
            depth -= text.startswith("}")
            if depth == 0:
                return index
            depth += text.endswith("{")
        return None

    def mutant(
        self,
        index: int,
        cols: tuple[int, int],
        rank: int,
        new: str,
        description: str,
        group: int,
        end_index: int | None = None,
        late: bool = False,
    ) -> Mutant:
        """Line `index` from `cols[0]` to line `end_index` (default `index`) at
        `cols[1]` becomes `new`."""
        end = self.offset(index if end_index is None else end_index, cols[1])
        return Mutant(
            self.path,
            index + 1,
            self.starts[group],
            (rank, cols[0]),
            late,
            self.offset(index, cols[0]),
            end,
            new.encode(),
            description,
        )

    def deletion(self, first: int, last: int, end_col: int) -> Mutant:
        """The command becomes `:`."""
        line = self.lines[first]
        text = self.text(first, last)
        return self.mutant(
            first,
            (len(line) - len(line.lstrip()), end_col),
            DELETE,
            ":",
            f"deleted: {short(text)}",
            first,
            end_index=last,
            late=SHELL_LATE.match(text) is not None,
        )

    def negation(self, index: int, condition: re.Match[str]) -> Mutant:
        """`! C`, `! { C; }` for a list or a pipeline, and `! C` becomes C."""
        test = condition[2]
        if test.startswith("! "):
            new = test[2:].lstrip()
        elif re.search(r"&&|\|", test):
            new = f"! {{ {test}; }}"
        else:
            new = f"! {test}"
        cols = condition.span(2)
        return self.mutant(index, cols, NEGATE, new, f"negated: {short(test)}", index)

    def comparisons(self, index: int) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
        """= or == and !=, -eq and -ne swap inside [ ] or [[ ]]."""
        line = self.lines[index]
        for test in SHELL_TEST.finditer(line):
            for op in SHELL_COMPARISON.finditer(line, test.start(), test.end()):
                new = SHELL_OPPOSITE[op[1]]
                description = f"{op[1]} -> {new}: {short(test[0])}"
                yield self.mutant(index, op.span(), SWAP, new, description, index)

    def defaults(self, first: int, last: int) -> Iterator[Mutant]:
        """W in ${N:-W}, ${N-W}, ${N:=W} or ${N=W} becomes XXWXX."""
        for index in range(first, last + 1):
            for default in SHELL_DEFAULT.finditer(self.lines[index]):
                word = f"XX{default[1]}XX"
                head = default[0][: default.start(1) - default.start()]
                description = f"{default[0]} -> {head}{word}}}"
                cols = default.span(1)
                yield self.mutant(index, cols, STRING, word, description, first)


def continues(line: str) -> bool:
    text = line.rstrip()
    return not text.lstrip().startswith("#") and text.endswith(CONTINUED)


def shell_parses(text: bytes) -> bool:
    assert BASH is not None
    checked = subprocess.run([BASH, "-n"], input=text, capture_output=True, check=False)
    return checked.returncode == 0


MUTATORS = {PYTHON: PythonFile, SHELL: ShellFile}
PARSES = {PYTHON: python_parses, SHELL: shell_parses}


def shebang_kind(path: Path) -> str | None:
    """PYTHON or SHELL by the file's #! line: a python, or `uv run ... --script`, or
    sh, bash or dash, also through env."""
    try:
        with path.open("rb") as f:
            first = f.readline(256)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not first.startswith(b"#!"):
        return None
    words = first[2:].decode(errors="replace").split()
    if words and PurePosixPath(words[0]).name == "env":
        words = list(itertools.dropwhile(env_option, words[1:]))
    if not words:
        return None
    program = PurePosixPath(words[0]).name
    if re.fullmatch(r"python[\d.]*", program) or (
        program == "uv" and "--script" in words
    ):
        return PYTHON
    return SHELL if program in ("sh", "bash", "dash") else None


def env_option(word: str) -> bool:
    """An option or a variable that env takes before the program."""
    return word.startswith("-") or "=" in word


def code_kind(path: Path, name: str) -> str | None:
    """PYTHON or SHELL for a code file, by its name or else its #! line; None for
    tests, their helpers, and anything that isn't code."""
    parts = PurePosixPath(name)
    folders_and_stem = (*parts.parent.parts, parts.stem)
    if parts.name == "conftest.py" or any(map(TEST_PART.search, folders_and_stem)):
        return None
    if path.is_symlink() or not path.is_file():
        return None
    if parts.suffix == ".py":
        return PYTHON
    if parts.suffix in (".sh", ".bash"):
        return SHELL
    return None if parts.suffix else shebang_kind(path)


def git(*args: str) -> str:
    """git's output. Raises UsageError with its message when it fails."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "--literal-pathspecs", *args], capture_output=True, check=False
        )
    except OSError as e:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: --diff needs git: {e.strerror}") from e
    if done.returncode != 0:
        said = done.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: git {args[0]}: {said}")
    return os.fsdecode(done.stdout)


def added_lines(patch: str) -> set[int]:
    """The numbers, in the new file, of the lines a patch adds."""
    added: set[int] = set()
    number = None
    for line in patch.split("\n"):
        hunk = HUNK.match(line)
        if hunk:
            number = int(hunk[1])
        elif number is not None and line.startswith(("+", " ")):
            if line.startswith("+"):
                added.add(number)
            number += 1
    return added


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class CodeFile:
    path: Path
    kind: str
    original: Original


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Diff:
    """The code a diff changed: each file and its mutants, the commit it starts from,
    and how many code files it changed but has no mutants for."""

    files: dict[Path, CodeFile]
    mutants: list[Mutant]
    start: str
    not_mutated: int


def read_diff(base: str) -> Diff:
    """The mutants of the lines that the diff since BASE's merge base with HEAD adds or
    changes, work tree included. Prints a line for each code file left without. Raises
    UsageError when git can't make the diff."""
    top = Path(git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel").strip())
    try:
        commit = git(
            "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "--end-of-options", base + "^{commit}"
        )
    except UsageError:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: --diff {base}: not a commit") from None
    start = git("merge-base", commit.strip(), "HEAD").strip()
    listed = git("diff", "--name-status", "-z", "-M", "--no-relative", start, "--")
    fields = iter(listed.split("\0"))
    files: dict[Path, CodeFile] = {}
    mutants: list[Mutant] = []
    reasons: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    for status in fields:
        if not status:
            continue
        paths = [next(fields), next(fields)] if status[0] in "RC" else [next(fields)]
        path = top / paths[-1]
        kind = code_kind(path, paths[-1])
        if status[0] in "DU" or kind is None:
            continue
        patch = git(
            *("diff", "-U0", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff", "--no-textconv", "-M"),
            *("--no-relative", start, "--", *(str(top / p) for p in paths)),
        )
        changed = added_lines(patch)
        if not changed:
            continue
        text = path.read_bytes()
        try:
            found = MUTATORS[kind](path, text, changed).mutants()
        except NotMutated as e:
            reasons.setdefault(str(e), []).append(os.path.relpath(path))
            continue
        files[path] = CodeFile(path, kind, Original(text, path.stat().st_mode))
        mutants += found
    untracked = git("-C", str(top), "ls-files", "-z", "--others", "--exclude-standard")
    names = [name for name in untracked.split("\0") if name]
    left = [os.path.relpath(top / n) for n in names if code_kind(top / n, n)]
    if left:
        print(
            f"mutate-check: untracked, so not mutated (git add -N brings one in): {', '.join(left)}"
        )
    for reason, names in reasons.items():
        print(f"mutate-check: {reason}, so not mutated: {', '.join(names)}")
    not_mutated = sum(map(len, reasons.values()))
    return Diff(files, mutants, start, not_mutated)


def in_turn(diff: Diff) -> list[Mutant]:
    """The mutants in the order they run: each statement's first, in diff order, then
    each one's second, and so on; then the late ones, the same way."""
    files = {path: index for index, path in enumerate(diff.files)}
    order = []
    for late in (False, True):
        groups: dict[tuple[int, int], list[Mutant]] = {}
        for mutant in diff.mutants:
            if mutant.late == late:
                key = (files[mutant.path], mutant.group)
                groups.setdefault(key, []).append(mutant)
        queues = [
            sorted(group, key=lambda m: m.rank) for _, group in sorted(groups.items())
        ]
        order += [m for turn in itertools.zip_longest(*queues) for m in turn if m]
    return order


class Unjudged(Exception):
    """The command fails or hangs with no mutation, so no mutant can be judged."""


@dataclass
class Tally:
    caught: int = 0
    survived: int = 0
    inconclusive: int = 0
    skipped: int = 0
    left_out: int = 0
    # Mutants an early stop kept from running.
    not_run: int = 0

    def judged(self) -> int:
        return self.caught + self.survived + self.inconclusive

    def summary(self, limit: int) -> str:
        line = (
            f"mutate-check: {self.caught} caught, {self.survived} survived,"
            f" {self.inconclusive} inconclusive, {self.skipped} skipped (didn't parse)"
        )
        if self.left_out:
            line += f"; {self.left_out} left out by --max {limit}"
        return line + (f"; {self.not_run} not run" if self.not_run else "")


def baseline(
    command: list[str], timeout: float | None, diff: Diff, least: float
) -> float:
    """Run the command with no mutation, and give each mutant's timeout: `timeout`, or
    TIMEOUT_FACTOR times this run, at least `least` seconds. Raises Unjudged unless the
    judge reads the run as a pass with tests run, and UsageError when the command won't
    start or rewrites a code file."""
    allowed = timeout or DEFAULT_TIMEOUT
    started = time.monotonic()
    said = "mutate-check: no mutant ran: unmutated, the command has to pass with tests"
    try:
        code, output = run(command, allowed)
    except TimedOut:
        raise Unjudged(f"{said} run, and it hung past {allowed:g} seconds") from None
    except OSError as e:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: can't run {command[0]}: {e}") from e
    took = time.monotonic() - started
    rewritten = {
        f.path: f.original for f in diff.files.values() if not holds_original(f)
    }
    if rewritten:
        failed = restore(rewritten)
        names = ", ".join(map(os.path.relpath, rewritten))
        said = (
            f"mutate-check: the command rewrote {names} with no mutation, so the"
            " mutants' places are wrong; give a command that leaves the code alone"
        )
        raise UsageError("\n".join([said, *failed]))
    verdict = judge(code, output, command)
    if verdict.status != MISSED:
        raise Unjudged(
            "\n".join([f"{said} run, and {verdict.reason}", *verdict.report[1:]])
        )
    return timeout or max(least, TIMEOUT_FACTOR * took)


def holds_original(file: CodeFile) -> bool:
    try:
        return file.path.read_bytes() == file.original.content
    except OSError:
        return False


def mutant_run(
    file: CodeFile, text: bytes, command: list[str], timeout: float
) -> Verdict:
    """Run the command with the file holding `text`, put the file back, and give the
    verdict, inconclusive for a hang. Raises Stopped, and UsageError when the file
    can't be written or put back, or the command won't start."""
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, STOP_SIGNALS)
    try:
        try:
            put(file.path, Original(text, file.original.mode))
        except OSError as e:
            raise UsageError(
                f"mutate-check: couldn't write a mutant to {file.path}: {e}"
            )
        # A stop that came while the file was being written lands here, inside the try.
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, STOP_SIGNALS)
        verdict = judge(*run(command, timeout), command)
    except TimedOut:
        hung = f"the command hung past {timeout:g} seconds"
        verdict = Verdict(INCONCLUSIVE, [f"mutate-check: inconclusive: {hung}"], hung)
    except OSError as e:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: can't run {command[0]}: {e}") from e
    finally:
        failed = restore({file.path: file.original})
    if failed:
        raise UsageError("\n".join(failed))
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, STOP_SIGNALS)
    return verdict


def run_mutants(
    diff: Diff, command: list[str], timeout: float, limit: int, tally: Tally
) -> None:
    """Run the command once for each mutant, up to `limit`, counting the verdicts and
    printing a line for each that survived. When the first EARLY_STOP are all
    inconclusive, the rest would most likely be too: it stops, and prints the last
    one's verdict."""
    seen = set()
    queue = in_turn(diff)
    for place, mutant in enumerate(queue):
        file = diff.files[mutant.path]
        text = mutant.applied_to(file.original.content)
        digest = hashlib.sha256(text).digest()
        if (mutant.path, digest) in seen:
            continue
        seen.add((mutant.path, digest))
        if tally.judged() == limit:
            tally.left_out += 1
        elif not PARSES[file.kind](text):
            tally.skipped += 1
        else:
            verdict = mutant_run(file, text, command, timeout)
            if verdict.status == MISSED:
                tally.survived += 1
                where = f"{os.path.relpath(mutant.path)}:{mutant.line}"
                print(f"{where}: {mutant.description}", flush=True)
            elif verdict.status == CAUGHT:
                tally.caught += 1
            else:
                tally.inconclusive += 1
            if tally.inconclusive == tally.judged() == EARLY_STOP:
                print(
                    f"mutate-check: stopped: the first {EARLY_STOP} mutants were all"
                    " inconclusive; the last one's run:"
                )
                for line in verdict.report:
                    print(f"  {line.removeprefix('mutate-check: ')}")
                tally.not_run = len(queue) - place - 1
                return


def diff_main(options: Options) -> int:
    assert options.base is not None
    limit = options.limit or DEFAULT_MAX
    tally = Tally()
    for signum in STOP_SIGNALS:
        signal.signal(signum, stop)
    try:
        least = seconds(os.environ.get("MUTATE_CHECK_MIN_TIMEOUT"), MIN_TIMEOUT)
        diff = read_diff(options.base)
        if not diff.mutants and diff.not_mutated:
            print("mutate-check: nothing to mutate in the changed code it could read")
            return INCONCLUSIVE
        if not diff.mutants:
            head = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
            uncommitted = "; it's HEAD, so only uncommitted changes count"
            print(
                f"mutate-check: no Python or shell line changed since {options.base},"
                f" so nothing to mutate{uncommitted if diff.start == head else ''}"
            )
            return CAUGHT
        timeout = baseline(options.command, options.timeout, diff, least)
        run_mutants(diff, options.command, timeout, limit, tally)
    except UsageError as e:
        print(e, file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    except Unjudged as e:
        print(e)
        return INCONCLUSIVE
    except Stopped as e:
        print(tally.summary(limit))
        print(f"mutate-check: stopped by signal {e.signum}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 128 + e.signum
    print(tally.summary(limit))
    if tally.survived:
        return MISSED
    return CAUGHT if tally.caught else INCONCLUSIVE


def seconds(text: str | None, default: float) -> float:
    """A number of seconds, finite and over 0, or `default` for None. Raises
    ValueError."""
    if text is None:
        return default
    try:
        value = float(text)
    except ValueError:
        value = 0.0
    if not (math.isfinite(value) and value > 0):
        raise ValueError(text)
    return value


def parse_args(args: list[str]) -> Options:
    """The options before `--`, in any order, and the command. Raises UsageError."""
    timeout, edits_file, base, limit = None, None, None, None
    while args[:1] in (["--timeout"], ["--edits"], ["--diff"], ["--max"]):
        if len(args) < 2 or args[1] == "--":
            raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: {args[0]} takes a value")
        option, value = args[:2]
        if option == "--edits":
            edits_file = Path(value)
        elif option == "--diff":
            base = value
        elif option == "--max":
            limit = int(value) if value.isascii() and value.isdigit() else 0
            if limit < 1:
                raise UsageError(
                    "mutate-check: --max takes a number of mutants, 1 or more"
                )
        else:
            try:
                timeout = seconds(value, DEFAULT_TIMEOUT)
            except ValueError:
                raise UsageError(
                    "mutate-check: --timeout takes a number of seconds"
                ) from None
        args = args[2:]
    if args[:1] != ["--"] or len(args) < 2:
        raise UsageError(f"mutate-check: give the command after --\n{__doc__}")
    if limit is not None and base is None:
        raise UsageError("mutate-check: --max goes with --diff")
    if base is not None and edits_file is not None:
        raise UsageError(
            "mutate-check: --edits goes without --diff, which makes its own mutants"
        )
    return Options(args[1:], timeout, edits_file, base, limit)


def main() -> int:
    if sys.argv[1:2] in (["-h"], ["--help"]):
        print(__doc__)
        return 0
    try:
        options = parse_args(sys.argv[1:])
        if options.base is not None:
            return diff_main(options)
        command, edits_file = options.command, options.edits_file
        timeout = options.timeout or DEFAULT_TIMEOUT
        edits = edits_file.read_text() if edits_file else sys.stdin.read()
    except UsageError as e:
        print(e, file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"mutate-check: can't read {edits_file}: {e.strerror}", file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        print(f"mutate-check: {edits_file or 'stdin'} isn't UTF-8", file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    try:
        files = FORMAT.parse(edits)
    except FORMAT.UsageError as e:
        print(f"apply-edits: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    originals = snapshot([f.path for f in files])
    for signum in STOP_SIGNALS:
        signal.signal(signum, stop)
    status: int | None = None
    try:
        code, output = mutated_run(edits, command, timeout)
    except UsageError as e:
        print(e, file=sys.stderr)
        status = USAGE
    except TimedOut:
        print(
            f"mutate-check: inconclusive: the command hung past {timeout:g} seconds"
            " and was stopped; a hang isn't a catch"
        )
        status = INCONCLUSIVE
    except Stopped as e:
        print(f"mutate-check: stopped by signal {e.signum}", file=sys.stderr)
        status = 128 + e.signum
    finally:
        failed = restore(originals)
    if failed:
        print("\n".join(failed), file=sys.stderr)
        return USAGE
    if status is not None:
        return status
    verdict = judge(code, output, command)
    print("\n".join(verdict.report))
    return verdict.status


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
